"reference request - What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit? - MathOverflow (https://mathoverflow.net/questions/420897/what-is-the-smallest-size-of-a-shape-in-which-all-fixed-n-polyominos-can-fit)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1search0\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 381\nL0: #### 184 communities for the curious\nL1: \nL2: MathOverflow is part of Stack Overflow’s open communities: specialist spaces where curiosity is welcome, knowledge is shared freely, and the best answers rise to the top.\nL3: \nL4: 【0†Find your community†stackexchange.com】\nL5: \nL6: [Input: Search on MathOverflow…]\nL7: \nL8:   1.   2.   3.   4.   5. 【1†Log in】\nL9:   6. 【2†Sign up】\nL10:   1.     1. 【3†Home】\nL11:     2. 【4†Questions】\nL12:     3. 【5†Unanswered】\nL13:     4. 【6†AI Assist†stackoverflow.com】\nL14:     5. 【7†Tags】\nL15:     6.     7. 【8†Chat†chat.stackexchange.com】\nL16:     8. 【9†Users】\nL17:     9. # 【10†What is the smallest size of a shape in which all fixed $n$-polyominos can fit?】\nL18: \nL19: 【11†Ask Question】\nL20: \nL21: Asked 4 years, 6 months ago\nL22: \nL23: Modified 【12†4 years, 5 months ago】\nL24: \nL25: Viewed 1k times\nL26: \nL27: [Input]\nL28: \nL29: 26\nL30: \nL31: [Input]\nL32: Let $n$ be an integer and consider all fixed $n$-polyominos, i.e., without rotation or reflection. I am interested in finding a shape in which all polyominos can embed. (It is OK if multiple polyominos overlap.)\nL33: \nL34: For instance, for $n=3$, the fixed 3-polyominos are:\nL35: \nL36:     `###  #..  ##.  ##.  #..  .#.\nL37:     ...  #..  #..  .#.  ##.  ##.\nL38:     ...  #..  ...  ...  ...  ...\nL39:     `\nL40: \nL41: and these polyominos all embed in the following shape with 5 cells, which is the best possible:\nL42: \nL43:     `.#.\nL44:     ###\nL45:     .#.\nL46:     `\nL47: More generally, a suitable shape for arbitrary $n$ is the $n \\times n$ square (with $n^2$ cells) and a naive lower bound would be $2n-1$ cells (necessary to embed the horizontal and vertical line $n$-polyomino).\nL48: \nL49: I define an integer sequence $S_n$ to be the minimal number of cells of a shape in which all $n$-polyominos embed, and I am interested in understanding this sequence. In particular, specific questions are:\nL50:   * Can we always find an optimal shape that fits into an $n \\times n$ square? (this seems intuitively reasonable but I do not know how to prove it)\nL51:   * Can we prove that, asymptotically, $S_n = \\Theta(n^2)$? (the challenge is to show an $\\Omega(n^2)$ lower bound -- maybe this is already possible by simply looking at a subset of the polyominos, but I couldn't see how to do it)\nL52: \nL53: More generally, has this sequence already been studied?\nL54: To understand what happens here, I was able to compute by bruteforce computer search the first values of $S_n$, making the assumption that optimal shapes always fit in an $n$ by $n$ square (first point above) -- these values may turn out not to be optimal if this assumption is wrong:\nL55: \nL56:   * We have $S_1 = 1$, $S_2 = 3$ (easily), and $S_3 = 5$ (see above)\nL57:   * We have $S_4=9$ with a surprising shape:\nL58: \nL59:     `..#.\nL60:     .##.\nL61:     ####\nL62:     .##.\nL63:     `\nL64:   * We have $S_5 = 13$, with the unsurprising shape:\nL65: \nL66:     `..#..\nL67:     .###.\nL68:     #####\nL69:     .###.\nL70:     ..#..\nL71:     `\nL72: \nL73:   * We have $S_6 = 18$ with a surprising shape:\nL74: \nL75:     `..##..\nL76:     ..##..\nL77:     ######\nL78:     #####.\nL79:     ..##..\nL80:     ..#...\nL81:     `\nL82: \nL83:   * We have $S_7 = 24$, the shape is similar to $n=5$ but with a hole:\nL84: \nL85:     `...#...\nL86:     ..###..\nL87:     .#.###.\nL88:     #######\nL89:     .#####.\nL90:     ..###..\nL91:     ...#...\nL92:     `\nL93: \nL94:   * I do not know $S_8$\nL95: There are matching sequences for these terms in OEIS, but their definitions do not seem relevant... Edit: maybe 【13†https://oeis.org/A203567†oeis.org】 【14†https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0012365X01003570†www.sciencedirect.com】 would be worth investigating.\nL96: \nL97: Acknowledgement: This question is by 【15†Thomas Colcombet†www.irif.fr】 and 【16†Antonio Casares†www.labri.fr】.\nL98: \nL99: Edit: fixed the values of $S_5$ and $S_7$, many thanks to @RobPratt for noticing and reporting the errors!\nL100:   * 【17†reference-request】\nL101:   * 【18†co.combinatorics】\nL102:   * 【19†combinatorial-optimization】\nL103:   * 【20†integer-sequences】\nL104:   * 【21†extremal-combinatorics】\nL105: \nL106: [Button: Cite]\nL107: \nL108: 【22†Improve this question】\nL109: \nL110: Follow [Input]\nL111: \nL112: 【23†edited May 5, 2022 at 16:16】\nL113: \nL114: asked Apr 22, 2022 at 21:57\nL115: \nL116: 【24†a3nm】\nL117: \nL118: 592 3 3 silver badges 15 15 bronze badges\nL119:   * Seems like the \"straight polyominos\" (those obtained as a union of the squares touching a given segment, which need not be horizontal or vertical) should give the $\\Omega(n^2)$ lower bound, I'll write something tomorrow if no one has answered that part of the question\nL120: \nL121: –  【25†Saúl RM】\nL122: \nL123: Commented Apr 22, 2022 at 22:22\nL124: \nL125:   * You can at least get $S_n=\\Omega(n\\log n)$ by a simple argument based on the rectangles.\nL126: \nL127: –  【26†Steven Stadnicki】\nL128: \nL129: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 1:56\nL130: \nL131:   * 2\nL132: The case of the \"straight polyominos\" proposed by Saúl is the digital equivalent of the celebrated Kakeya problem: 【27†en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kakeya_set†en.wikipedia.org】\nL133: \nL134: –  【28†domotorp】\nL135: \nL136: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 5:13\nL137: \nL138:   * 2\nL139: @a3nm Take a nice set $S$ whose area is $<0.01n^2$ and contains a length $n$ segment in every direction. Such a set $S$ exists by the constructions given on Wikipeida for the Kakeya set. Now pixelize $S$ by checking which unit squares it intersects from the unit grid. The number of these squares is $0.01n^2 +O(n)$ as the perimeter of $S$ is nice. As $n\\to \\infty$, this gives an upper bound of $0.01n^2$, and we can pick $0.01$ as small as we want to.\nL140: \nL141: –  【28†domotorp】\nL142: \nL143: Commented Apr 23, 2022 at 8:53\nL144: \nL145:   * 1\nL146: I've asked about something similar about free polyominoes on 【29†Code Golf Stack Exchange†codegolf.stackexchange.com】 and 【30†Math Stack Exchange†math.stackexchange.com】.\nL147: \nL148: –  【31†Peter Kagey】\nL149: \nL150: Commented Apr 24, 2022 at 2:11\nL151: \nL152:  |  【32†Show 17 more comments】\nL153: ## 3 Answers\nL154: \nL155: Sorted by:\nL156: \nL157: [Select]\nL158: \nL159: [Input]\nL160: \nL161: 9\nL162: \nL163: [Input]\nL164: It is actually $\\ge cn^2$ with some $c>0$. The value of $c$ I'll obtain is pretty dismal but I tried to trade the precision for the argument simplicity everywhere I could, so it can be certainly improved quite a bit. I have no doubt that it is written somewhere (perhaps, in the continuous form: the $2$-dimensional measure of a set containing a shift of every rectifiable curve of length $1$ is at least some positive constant) but I'll leave it to more educated people to provide the reference.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.csun.edu/~ctoth/Handbook/chap14.pdf)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 22; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":95}); Total lines: 1139\nL48@P1: Translation-equivalent: Sets S, S′ of cells such that S\nL49@P1: ′\nL50@P1: is a translate of S.\nL51@P1: Fixed polyomino: A translation-equivalence class of polyominoes; t(n) denotes\nL52@P1: the number of fixed n-ominoes. ((A(n) is also widely used in the literature.)\nL53@P1: Representatives of the six fixed 3-ominoes are shown in Figure 14.2.1.\nL54@P1: FIGURE 14.2.1\nL55@P1: The six fixed 3-ominoes.\nL56@P1: The lexicographic cell ordering ≺ on Z\nL57@P1: 2\nL58@P1: is defined by: [r, s] ≺ [u, v] if s < v, or\nL59@P1: if s = v and r < u.\nL60@P1: Standard position: The translate S −(u, v) of S, where [u, v] is the lexicograph\u0002ically minimum cell in S.\nL61@P1: A finite set S ⊂ Z\nL62@P1: 2\nL63@P1: is in standard position if and only if [0, 0] ∈ S, v ≥ 0 for all\nL64@P1: [u, v] ∈ S, and u ≥ 0 for all [u, 0] ∈ S.\nL65@P1: Rotation-translation group: The group R of mappings of Z\nL66@P1: 2\nL67@P1: to itself of the\nL68@P1: form [u, v] 7→ [u, v]\nL69@P1: \u0014\nL70@P1: 0 −1\nL71@P1: 1 0 \u0015k\nL72@P1: + (r, s). (The matrix \u0014\nL73@P1: 0 −1\nL74@P1: 1 0 \u0015\nL75@P1: , which is de\u0002noted by R, maps [u, v] to [v, −u] by right multiplication, hence represents a\nL76@P1: clockwise rotation of 90◦.)\nL77@P1: Rotationally equivalent: Sets S, S′ of cells with S\nL78@P1: ′ = ρS for some ρ ∈ R.\nL79@P1: Chiral polyomino, or handed polyomino: A rotational-equivalence class of\nL80@P1: polyominoes; r(n) denotes the number of chiral n-ominoes.\nL81@P1: The top row of 5-ominoes in Figure 14.2.2 consists of the set of cells F = {[0, −1],\nL82@P1: [−1, 0], [0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1]}, together with F R, F R2, and F R3. All four of these\nL83@P1: 5-ominoes are rotationally equivalent. The bottom row in Figure 14.2.2 shows\nL84@P1: these same four 5-ominoes reflected about the x-axis. These four 5-ominoes are\nL85@P1: rotationally equivalent as well, but none of them is rotationally equivalent to\nL86@P1: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL87@P1-2: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.Chapter 14: Polyominoes 361\nL88@P2: any of the 5-ominoes shown in the top row. Representatives of the seven chiral\nL89@P2: 4-ominoes are shown in Figure 14.2.3.\nL90@P2: FIGURE 14.2.2\nL91@P2: The 5-ominoes in the top row are\nL92@P2: rotationally equivalent, and so are their\nL93@P2: reflections in the bottom row, but the\nL94@P2: two sets are rotationally distinct. FM FRM FR2M FR3M\nL95@P2: F FR FR FR 2 3\nL96@P2: FIGURE 14.2.3\nL97@P2: The seven chiral 4-ominoes.\nL98@P2: Congruence group:\nL99@P2: \u0014\nL100@P2: The group S of motions generated by the matrix M =\nL101@P2: 1 0\nL102@P2: 0 −1\nL103@P2: \u0015\nL104@P2: (reflection in the x-axis) and the rotation-translation group R. (A\nL105@P2: typical element of S has the form [u, v] 7→ [u, v]RkMi+(r, s), for some k = 0, 1, 2,\nL106@P2: or 3, some i = 0 or 1, and some r, s ∈ Z.)\nL107@P2: Congruent: Sets S and S\nL108@P2: ′ of cells such that S′ = σ(S) for some σ ∈ S.\nL109@P2: Free polyomino: A congruence class of polyominoes; s(n) denotes the number\nL110@P2: of free n-ominoes. The twelve free 5-ominoes are shown in Figure 14.2.4.\nL111@P2: THEOREM 14.2.1 Embedding Theorem\nL112@P2: For each n, let Un consist of the n\nL113@P2: 2 − n + 1 cells of the form [u, v], where\nL114@P2: \u001a\nL115@P2: 0 ≤ u ≤ n, for v = 0\nL116@P2: |u| + v ≤ n, for v > 0\nL117@P2: . (See Figure 14.2.5 for the case n = 5.) Then, all\nL118@P2: n-ominoes in standard position are edge-connected subsets of Un that contain [0, 0].\nL119@P2: COROLLARY 14.2.2\nL120@P2: The number of fixed n-ominoes is finite for each n.\nL121@P2: The same result can be obtained by a simple argument due to Eden [Ede61]:\nL122@P2: Every polyomino P of size n can be built according to a set of n−1 “instructions”\nL123@P2: taken from a superset of size 3(n−1). Starting with a single square, each instruction\nL124@P2: tells us how to choose a lattice cell c, neighboring a cell already in P, and add c to P.\nL125@P2: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL126@P2-3: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.362 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL127@P3: FIGURE 14.2.4\nL128@P3: The twelve free 5-ominoes.\nL129@P3: FIGURE 14.2.5\nL130@P3: A set of n\nL131@P3: 2 −n+ 1 cells (for n = 5) that contains\nL132@P3: every n-omino in standard position.\nL133@P3: x\nL134@P3: y\nL135@P3: (Some of these instruction sets are illegal, and some other sets produce the same\nL136@P3: polyominoes.) Hence, the number of polyominoes of size n is less than\nL137@P3: 3(n−1)\nL138@P3: n−1\nL139@P3: \u0001\nL140@P3: .\nL141@P3: 14.3 HOW MANY n-OMINOES ARE THERE?\nL142@P3: Table 14.3.1, calculated by Redelmeier [Red81], indicates the values of t(n), r(n),\nL143@P3: and s(n) for n = 1, . . . , 24. Jensen and Guttmann [JG00, Jen01] and Jensen [Jen03]\nL144@P3: extended the enumeration of polyominoes up to n = 56. See also sequence A001168\nL145@P3: in the OEIS (On-line Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences) [oeis].\nL146@P3: It is easy to see that for each n, we have\nL147@P3: t(n)\nL148@P3: 8\nL149@P3: ≤ s(n) ≤ r(n) ≤ t(n).\nL150@P3: The values of t(n) seem to be growing exponentially, and indeed they have expo\u0002nential bounds.\nL151@P3: THEOREM 14.3.1 [Kla67]\nL152@P3: limn→∞(t(n))1/n = λ exists.\nL153@P3: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL154@P3-4: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.Chapter 14: Polyominoes 363\nL155@P4: TABLE 14.3.1 The number of fixed, chiral, and\nL156@P4: free n-ominoes for n ≤ 24.\nL157@P4: n t(n) r(n) s(n)\nL158@P4: 1 1 1 1\nL159@P4: 2 2 1 1\nL160@P4: 3 6 2 2\nL161@P4: 4 19 7 5\nL162@P4: 5 63 18 12\nL163@P4: 6 216 60 35\nL164@P4: 7 760 196 108\nL165@P4: 8 2725 704 369\nL166@P4: 9 9910 2500 1285\nL167@P4: 10 36446 9189 4655\nL168@P4: 11 135268 33896 17073\nL169@P4: 12 505861 126759 63600\nL170@P4: 13 1903890 476270 238591\nL171@P4: 14 7204874 1802312 901971\nL172@P4: 15 27394666 6849777 3426576\nL173@P4: 16 104592937 26152418 13079255\nL174@P4: 17 400795844 100203194 50107909\nL175@P4: 18 1540820542 385221143 192622052\nL176@P4: 19 5940738676 1485200848 742624232\nL177@P4: 20 22964779660 5741256764 2870671950\nL178@P4: 21 88983512783 22245940545 11123060678\nL179@P4: 22 345532572678 86383382827 43191857688\nL180@P4: 23 1344372335524 336093325058 168047007728\nL181@P4: 24 5239988770268 1309998125640 654999700403\nL182@P4: This constant (often referred to in the literature as the “growth constant” of\nL183@P4: polyominoes) has since then been called “Klarner’s constant.” Only three decades\nL184@P4: later, Madras proved the existence of the asymptotic growth ratio.\nL185@P4: THEOREM 14.3.2 [Mad99]\nL186@P4: limn→∞ t(n + 1)/t(n) exists (and is hence equal to λ).\nL187@P4: The currently best known lower [BRS16] and upper [KR73] bounds on the\nL188@P4: constant λ are 4.0025 and 4.6496, respectively. The proof of the lower bound uses an\nL189@P4: analysis of polyominoes on twisted cylinders with the help of a supercomputer, and\nL190@P4: the proof of the upper bound uses a composition argument of so-called twigs. The\nL191@P4: currently best (unproved) estimate of λ, 4.0625696±0.0000005, is by Jensen [Jen03].\nL192@P4: ALGORITHMS\nL193@P4: Redelmeier\nL194@P4: Considerable effort has been expended to find a formula for the number of fixed n\u0002ominoes (say), with no success. Redelmeier’s algorithm, which produced the entries\nL195@P4: in Table 14.3.1, generates all fixed n-ominoes one by one and counts them. The\nL196@P4: Preliminary version (July 29, 2017). To appear in the Handbook of Discrete and Computational Geometry,\nL197@P4-5: J.E. Goodman, J. O'Rourke, and C. D. Tóth (editors), 3rd edition, CRC Press, Boca Raton, FL, 2017.364 G. Barequet, S.W. Golomb, and D.A. Klarner\nL198@P5: recursive algorithm searches G, the underlying cell-adjacency graph of the square\nL199@P5: lattice, and counts all connected subgraphs of G (up to a predetermined size) that\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncode challenge - Smallest region of the plane that contains all free n-ominoes - Code Golf Stack Exchange (https://codegolf.stackexchange.com/questions/167484/smallest-region-of-the-plane-that-contains-all-free-n-ominoes)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":1400}); Total lines: 2664\nL1348:                         if (!IsLocked(cs[i]) && IsSatisfied(cs[i])) Remove(cs[i]);\nL1349:                         else cs[j++] = cs[i];\nL1350:                     }\nL1351:                     cs.ShrinkTo(j);\nL1352:                 }\nL1353: \nL1354:                 SimpDBAssigns = nAssigns;\nL1355:                 SimpDBProps = ClausesLiterals + LearntsLiterals;\nL1356:             }\nL1357: \nL1358:             LBool Search(int nof_conflicts, int nof_learnts)\nL1359:             {\nL1360:                 if (!Ok) return l_False;\nL1361:                 Assert(0 == DecisionLevel);\nL1362:                 int conflictC = 0;\nL1363:                 Model.Clear();\nL1364: \nL1365:                 while (true)\nL1366:                 {\nL1367:                     Clause confl = Propagate();\nL1368:                     if (confl != null)\nL1369:                     {\nL1370:                         // CONFLICT\nL1371:                         conflictC++;\nL1372:                         var learnt_clause = new List<Lit>();\nL1373:                         if (DecisionLevel == 0) return l_False; // Contradiction found\nL1374:                         CancelUntil(Analyze(confl, learnt_clause));\nL1375:                         NewClause(learnt_clause, true);\nL1376:                         if (learnt_clause.Count == 1) Level[learnt_clause[0].Var] = 0;\nL1377:                         VarDecayActivity();\nL1378:                         ClaDecayActivity();\nL1379:                     }\nL1380:                     else\nL1381:                     {\nL1382:                         // NO CONFLICT\nL1383:                         if (nof_conflicts >= 0 && conflictC >= nof_conflicts)\nL1384:                         {\nL1385:                             // Reached bound on number of conflicts\nL1386:                             CancelUntil(0);\nL1387:                             return l_Undef;\nL1388:                         }\nL1389: \nL1390:                         // Simplify the set of problem clauses\nL1391:                         if (DecisionLevel == 0) { SimplifyDB(); if (!Ok) return l_False; }\nL1392: \nL1393:                         // Reduce the set of learnt clauses\nL1394:                         if (nof_learnts >= 0 && Learnts.Count - nAssigns >= nof_learnts) ReduceDB();\nL1395:                         // New variable decision\nL1396:                         Lit next = Order.Select(RandomVarFreq);\nL1397: \nL1398:                         if (next == lit_Undef)\nL1399:                         {\nL1400:                             // Model found\nL1401:                             Model.Clear();\nL1402:                             Model.Capacity = nVars;\nL1403:                             Model.AddRange(Assigns);\nL1404:                             CancelUntil(0);\nL1405:                             return l_True;\nL1406:                         }\nL1407:                         Check(Assume(next));\nL1408:                     }\nL1409:                 }\nL1410:             }\nL1411: \nL1412:             void VarRescaleActivity()\nL1413:             {\nL1414:                 for (int i = 0; i < nVars; i++) Activity[i] *= 1e-100f;\nL1415:                 VarInc *= 1e-100f;\nL1416:             }\nL1417: \nL1418:             void ClaRescaleActivity()\nL1419:             {\nL1420:                 for (int i = 0; i < Learnts.Count; i++) Learnts[i].Activity *= 1e-20f;\nL1421:                 ClaInc *= 1e-20;\nL1422:             }\nL1423: \nL1424:             public bool Solve()\nL1425:             {\nL1426:                 SimplifyDB();\nL1427:                 Assert(DecisionLevel == 0);\nL1428: \nL1429:                 double nof_conflicts = 100;\nL1430:                 double nof_learnts = nClauses / 3;\nL1431:                 while (true)\nL1432:                 {\nL1433:                     if (Search((int)nof_conflicts, (int)nof_learnts) != l_Undef)\nL1434:                     {\nL1435:                         CancelUntil(0);\nL1436:                         return Ok;\nL1437:                     }\nL1438:                     nof_conflicts *= 1.5;\nL1439:                     nof_learnts *= 1.1;\nL1440:                 }\nL1441:             }\nL1442: \nL1443:             #endregion\nL1444:         }\nL1445:     }\nL1446:     `\nL1447: ### Optimality\nL1448: \nL1449: The code above keeps reducing the target size until it finds an unsatisfiable constraint, so it guarantees that the output is optimal (under the bounding box assumption) up to and including \\$n=11\\$. However, it runs out of memory (3GB for a 32-bit process or 4GB for a 64-bit process) with \\$n=12\\$ after producing a region with weight 43.\nL1450: To run the search for \\$n=12\\$ to completion I found it necessary to reduce the memory considerably, special-casing binary clauses and not keeping empty watch lists. However, by changing the order in which clauses are considered it changes the results, so I present the change as a patch and leave the list of solutions above untouched.\nL1451: \nL1452:     `--- MiniSAT.cs.old\nL1453:     +++ MiniSAT.cs\nL1454:     @@ -346,6 +346,7 @@ namespace MiniSAT\nL1455:              const double RandomVarFreq = 0.02;\nL1456:              List<List<Clause>> Watches = new List<List<Clause>>(); // 'watches[lit]' is a list of constraints watching 'lit' (will go there if literal becomes true).\nL1457:     +        List<List<Lit>> BinaryWatches = new List<List<Lit>>();\nL1458:              public List<LBool> Assigns = new List<LBool>(); // The current assignments.\nL1459:              public List<Lit> Trail = new List<Lit>(); // Assignment stack; stores all assigments made in the order they were made.\nL1460:              List<int> TrailLim = new List<int>(); // Separator indices for different decision levels in 'trail'.\nL1461:     @@ -381,7 +382,9 @@ namespace MiniSAT\nL1462:              void Remove(Clause c)\nL1463:              {\nL1464:                  RemoveWatch(Watches[(~c[0]).Index], c);\nL1465:     +            if (Watches[(~c[0]).Index] != null && Watches[(~c[0]).Index].Count == 0) Watches[(~c[0]).Index] = null;\nL1466:                  RemoveWatch(Watches[(~c[1]).Index], c);\nL1467:     +            if (Watches[(~c[1]).Index] != null && Watches[(~c[1]).Index].Count == 0) Watches[(~c[1]).Index] = null;\nL1468: \nL1469:                  if (c.IsLearnt) LearntsLiterals -= c.Size;\nL1470:                  else ClausesLiterals -= c.Size;\nL1471:     @@ -408,6 +411,7 @@ namespace MiniSAT\nL1472:                  Level.Capacity = numVars;\nL1473:                  Reason.Capacity = numVars;\nL1474:                  Watches.Capacity = numVars << 1;\nL1475:     +            BinaryWatches.Capacity = numVars << 1;\nL1476:                  Order.Heap_.SetBounds(numVars + 1);\nL1477:                  Trail.Capacity = numVars;\nL1478:                  TrailPos.Capacity = numVars;\nL1479:     @@ -500,7 +504,7 @@ namespace MiniSAT\nL1480: \nL1481:                      if (!learnt)\nL1482:                      {\nL1483:     -                    Clauses.Add(c);\nL1484:     +                    if (c.Size > 2) Clauses.Add(c);\nL1485:                          ClausesLiterals += c.Size;\nL1486:                      }\nL1487:                      else\nL1488:     @@ -526,8 +530,20 @@ namespace MiniSAT\nL1489:                      }\nL1490: \nL1491:                      // Watch clause:\nL1492:     -                Watches[(~c[0]).Index].Push(c);\nL1493:     -                Watches[(~c[1]).Index].Push(c);\nL1494:     +                if (c.Size == 2 && !learnt)\nL1495:     +                {\nL1496:     +                    if (BinaryWatches[(~c[0]).Index] == null) BinaryWatches[(~c[0]).Index] = new List<Lit>();\nL1497:     +                    BinaryWatches[(~c[0]).Index].Push(c[1]);\nL1498:     +                    if (BinaryWatches[(~c[1]).Index] == null) BinaryWatches[(~c[1]).Index] = new List<Lit>();\nL1499:     +                    BinaryWatches[(~c[1]).Index].Push(c[0]);\nL1500:     +                }\nL1501:     +                else\nL1502:     +                {\nL1503:     +                    if (Watches[(~c[0]).Index] == null) Watches[(~c[0]).Index] = new List<Clause>();\nL1504:     +                    Watches[(~c[0]).Index].Push(c);\n"
